#: conf.json:summary
msgid "Algebraic traits and default numeric instances that define the generic foundation for LunaFlow math packages."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:1
msgid "core API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:3
#: manual/api/hom.md:3
msgid "Purpose"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:5
msgid "`luna-generic` is the algebraic trait layer of LunaFlow. It does not provide numerical algorithms or containers by itself; it defines the reusable capability surface that higher-level packages depend on."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:9
msgid "Structural traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:20
msgid "These traits live in `src/structure.mbt`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:22
msgid "Operational traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:29
msgid "These traits live in `src/operation.mbt`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:31
msgid "Shipped instances"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:33
msgid "Signed integers: `Int`, `Int16`, `Int64`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:34
msgid "Unsigned integers: `UInt`, `UInt16`, `UInt64`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:35
msgid "Exact big integer: `BigInt`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:36
msgid "Floating instances: `Float`, `Double`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:38
#: manual/api/hom.md:87
msgid "Semantic notes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:40
msgid "`Integral::normalize(Self) -> BigInt` is the canonical exact bridge from any integral instance into `BigInt`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:42
msgid "`Nat` reuses the same `normalize(Self) -> BigInt` bridge via trait inheritance."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:43
msgid "`NatHomomorphism::from_nat` and `IntegralHomomorphism::from_integral` are polymorphic embedding entry points over source traits instead of width-specific constructors."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:46
msgid "The default embedding strategy is `Integral::normalize` followed by target-specific conversion from `BigInt`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:48
msgid "Unsigned integer instances stop at `Semiring`; they do not pretend to be additive groups or rings."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:50
msgid "`Float` and `Double` implement homomorphism traits as approximate embeddings, so very large integral values may round."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:53
msgid "Source map"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:55
msgid "`src/structure.mbt`: trait definitions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:56
msgid "`src/operation.mbt`: operational traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:57
msgid "`src/impl_signed.mbt`, `src/impl_unsigned.mbt`, `src/impl_bigint.mbt`, `src/impl_float.mbt`, `src/impl_dbl.mbt`: default instances"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:1
msgid "hom API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:5
msgid "The `hom` subsystem provides generalized homomorphisms. A `Hom[S, A, B]` is a map `A -> B` that carries a certificate stating it preserves every operation of the signature `S`. The certificate cannot be forged outside this package, and the only unproven entry point is `Hom::postulate`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:10
msgid "Source: `src/hom.mbt`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:12
msgid "Signature tags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:20
msgid "Signature tags are empty enums that only appear at the type level. Users may define their own."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:23
msgid "Algebra dictionaries"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:25
msgid "`Op[A]`: one operation with `name`, `arity` and `eval`. `arity == 0` is a constant."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:26
msgid "`Algebra[S, A]`: an interpretation of the signature `S` on the carrier `A`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:27
msgid "`Algebra::add_monoid`, `mul_monoid`, `add_group`, `semiring`, `ring`: derived from the existing structure trait instances."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:29
msgid "`Algebra::make(ops)`: builds a dictionary for a custom signature tag."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:30
msgid "`Algebra::prod(a, b)`: the componentwise product algebra on `Prod[A, B]`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:32
msgid "Two dictionaries of the same `S` must list the same operations (name and arity) in the same order; otherwise `Algebra::prod` and `Hom::check_by` abort."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:35
msgid "Product type"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:37
msgid "`Prod[A, B]`: fields `fst` and `snd`. Implements `Add`, `Mul`, `Neg`, `Sub`, `Zero` and `One`, and `AddMonoid`, `MulMonoid`, `AddGroup`, `Semiring` and `Ring` whenever both components do."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:41
msgid "Homomorphisms"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:43
msgid "Trust entry (creates a proof obligation):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:45
msgid "`Hom::postulate(f)`: the caller promises that for every operation `op` of `S` and all arguments `xs`, `f(op_A(xs)) == op_B(xs.map(f))`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:48
msgid "Inference rules (no new obligation):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:50
msgid "`Hom::id()`, `h.then(g)`: identity and composition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:51
msgid "`h.forget(r)`: forgets structure along a `Reduct[S, T]` witness."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:52
msgid "`Hom::pair(f, g)`, `Hom::fst()`, `Hom::snd()`: pairing and projections."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:53
msgid "`h.to_add_group()`: an additive monoid hom between groups preserves `neg`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:54
msgid "`h.to_ring()`: a semiring hom between rings preserves `neg`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:56
msgid "Canonical embeddings (the obligation sits on the trait instance):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:58
msgid "`Hom::from_nat()`: backed by the target's `NatHomomorphism` instance, typed `Hom[SemiringSig, N, R]`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:60
msgid "`Hom::from_integral()`: backed by the target's `IntegralHomomorphism` instance, typed `Hom[SemiringSig, Z, R]`. Upgrade with `to_ring` when both sides are rings."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:64
msgid "Use:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:68
msgid "Reduct witnesses"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:75
msgid "Only this package constructs `Reduct` values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:77
msgid "Law checks"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:79
msgid "`h.check(src, dst, samples)`: checks the law with exact equality; requires `B : Eq`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:80
msgid "`h.check_by(src, dst, samples, rel)`: checks `rel(f(op_A(xs)), op_B(f(xs)))`. `rel` sets the strength of preservation: equality for strict homomorphisms, `<=` for lax ones such as subadditive maps, and a tolerance for approximate homomorphisms into floating-point targets."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:85
msgid "An operation of arity `n` is tested on `samples.length()^n` tuples."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:89
msgid "Fixed-width integer sources (`Int`, `Int64`, `UInt`, ...) stop being the integers once arithmetic wraps, so `Hom::from_integral` only holds on samples whose source arithmetic does not overflow."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/hom.md:92
msgid "`Float` and `Double` targets satisfy the laws only up to rounding; use `check_by` with a tolerance. See the embedding notes in the [core API](core.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:1
msgid "core design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:3
msgid "Design goal"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:5
msgid "`luna-generic` gives LunaFlow a shared algebraic vocabulary. Packages such as `arithmetic`, `luna-complex`, `linear-algebra`, and `luna-poly` should be able to describe their requirements in terms of reusable traits instead of inventing their own incompatible capability layers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:10
msgid "Main design decisions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:12
msgid "The trait graph is layered and intentionally small."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:13
msgid "Structural traits such as `Ring`, `Field`, `Integral`, and `Nat` are kept separate from operational traits such as `Zero`, `One`, `Inverse`, and `Conjugate`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:16
msgid "Embeddings are explicit through `NatHomomorphism` and `IntegralHomomorphism`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:18
msgid "`Integral::normalize` is the canonical exact bridge into `BigInt`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:19
msgid "Target-side default homomorphism implementations are expected to route through normalized `BigInt` values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:21
msgid "Unsigned types stop before additive inverse, so the abstraction stays mathematically honest."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:24
#: manual/design/hom.md:32
msgid "Boundaries"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:26
msgid "This package does not define matrices, complex numbers, polynomials, parsing, or numerical algorithms."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:28
msgid "It does not erase the semantic difference between exact and approximate number systems."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:1
msgid "hom design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:3
msgid "Goals"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:5
msgid "Express structure-preserving maps within MoonBit's current type system, hand the laws the type system cannot prove to the developer, and keep those obligations auditable and testable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:9
msgid "Constraints"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:11
msgid "MoonBit traits only have the `Self` parameter: there are no multi-parameter traits and no associated types, so a homomorphism `A -> B` cannot be a trait."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:13
msgid "Homomorphisms out of ℕ and ℤ are unique (initial objects), which is why `NatHomomorphism` and `IntegralHomomorphism` can live as target-side traits. Other homomorphisms are generally not unique and must be values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:17
msgid "Core decisions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:19
msgid "LCF-style certificates: `Hom[S, A, B]` has private fields and is only constructed inside this package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:21
msgid "The only public trust entry is `Hom::postulate`. Kernel rules use the package-private `trust`, so searching for `postulate` lists exactly the user-level obligations. (`assume` is a reserved word in MoonBit, hence the name.)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:25
msgid "The signature is a phantom type `S` on the certificate, while the algebra is passed as a dictionary value `Algebra[S, A]`: the certificate says what is preserved, the dictionary is used for checking."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:28
msgid "Signature inclusions are `Reduct[S, T]` witnesses only this package can build."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:29
msgid "The strength of preservation is the relation `rel` chosen at check time, so strict, lax and approximate homomorphisms share one API."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:34
msgid "Only single-sorted signatures. Multi-sorted structures such as modules (scalars plus vectors) are out of scope for this subsystem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:36
msgid "Without higher-kinded types, functorial lifts (polynomials, matrices, ...) belong to their own packages and are not generalized here."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/hom.md:38
msgid "Laws are tested, not proven. The certificate guarantees traceable origin, not that the laws hold."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:1
